== A Husband’s Providing for His Wife

=== §. 46. #emph[Of a husband’s provident care for his wife.]

A husband who tenderly respects his wife but has no provident care for her shows more affection than discretion: he may have a kind heart, but he lacks a wise head. How then can he be a good head to his wife? She may have some present contentment from him, but she can reap little profit and benefit from him. The duties that have been set out must therefore be done, but those that follow must by no means be left undone.

A husband’s provident care is noted in that office of Christ in which a husband resembles him, namely, to be a “Savior of #footnote[Eph 5:23.] the body,” as has been #footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 1, §. 15. & Treatise 3, §. 73.] declared before. It consists

+ In providing what is needful for his wife.
+ In protecting her from what is harmful.

1\. A careful #emph[providing of what is needful] is a principal part of #footnote[1\. Providing what is needful.] the #emph[honor] that husbands are to give to their wives.#footnote[1 Pet 3:7. 1 Tim 5:17.] For where the apostle says that “elders … are worthy of double honor,” he means #emph[maintenance] as well as #emph[reverence.] The apostle counts him “worse than an unbeliever” who “does not provide for his own,#footnote[1 Tim 5:8.] and especially his own household.” Who belongs to a husband’s household, if not his wife? In his house, who is more properly #emph[his own] than his wife? If then a husband does not provide for his wife, what is he #footnote[#emph[Recordare quòd pater vxoris tanquam depositum quoddam filiam tibi tradidit, & omnibus relictis, matre, domo, & seipso, omnem eius curam tuae dexterae commisit. Chrys. hom.] 26. #emph[in] 1 Cor 11. \[Remember that your wife’s father handed his daughter over to you as a kind of trust, and, with everything left behind, mother, home and himself, committed all the care of her to your right hand.\]] to be counted?

There is great reason he should provide for her, because he has taken her from her parents and family, and has received the portion they allotted her, and has authority committed to him over her, and she is put in subjection under him. Her family, having given away her portion and their power over her, and committed all to him, will take no further care for her; and she, being in subjection under him, cannot provide for herself without him. Who then will provide for her if he does not, whose she is wholly and only?

Contrary to this is the mind of those who take a wife only for their own #footnote[Contrary: to take a wife only for himself.] contentment, or delight, or gain, and never think of the charge they take on themselves together with a wife. Their practice matches their mind: for when they have a wife, they neglect her in everything except what may serve their own ends. They have much to answer for, and all the more because a wife is a special pledge of God’s favor. #footnote[Prov 18:22.]

=== §. 47. #emph[Of a husband’s providing means of spiritual edification for his wife.]

In this provident care that a husband ought to have for his wife, we will consider its extent and its continuance.

It ought to extend both to #emph[herself] and to #emph[others.]

With respect to herself, to her

- Soul.
- Body.

For her #emph[soul,] means of spiritual edification must be provided, both private and public. Private means are #footnote[Private means of edification.] holy and religious exercises in the house, such as reading the word, prayer, catechizing and the like. Since these are the spiritual food of the soul, they are to be provided and used every day, as our bodily food is. A husband, as master of a family, must provide these for the good of his whole house; but as a husband, especially for the good of his wife. For to his wife, as well as to the whole house, he is a king, a priest and a prophet.

He ought therefore to perform these things himself for his wife’s good, or to provide that they may be done by someone else. Cornelius himself performed those exercises. #footnote[Acts 10:2, 30. Judg 17:10.] Micah hired a Levite (though his idolatry was evil, yet his care to have a Levite in his house was commendable). The #footnote[2 Kgs 4:11.] Shunammite’s husband provided a room for the prophet, and that especially for his wife’s sake, for it was at her request.

Public means are the holy ordinances of God publicly #footnote[Public means of edification.] performed by God’s minister. A husband’s care for his wife in this respect is so to order his dwelling, and provide other needful things, that his wife may share in them. It is expressly noted of Elkanah that he so provided for his #footnote[1 Sam 1:7; 2:19. Luke 2:41.] wives that they went with him every year to the house of God; the same is implied of Joseph, the husband of the virgin Mary. In those days there was a public place and house of God, to which all God’s people (however far from it they lived) were to go every year. The places where Elkanah and Joseph lived were far away from the house of God, yet they so provided that not only they themselves but their wives also went to the public worship of God. Now there are many houses of God, places for the public worship of God; but even so, through the corruption of our times, the ministry of the word (the chief means of spiritual edification) is not to be had everywhere. Therefore a husband’s care for his wife in this respect ought to be such that he lives where she may have the benefit of the preaching of the word, or else so provides for her that she may go each week where it may be had.

If men of wisdom and means make a purchase, or build a house to live in, they will make sure it is where there are sweet rivers and waters, and good pasture ground, and where all needful provisions may be had. God’s word preached is a spring of the water of life; the place where it is preached is a pleasant, profitable pasture; all needful provision for the soul may be had there. Let this therefore be inquired after most of all, and let no dwelling be settled except where this may be had.

=== §. 48. #emph[Of neglecting their wives’ edification.]

Contrary to this is the practice of those who have their calling in places where the word is plentiful, yet for outward reasons of pleasure, delight, ease and profit move their families into remote places where preaching is scarce, if there is any at all. There they leave their wives to govern the family, with no regard for their lack of the word, since they themselves, often coming to London or other such places because of their calling, enjoy the word themselves. Many city tradesmen, lawyers and others are guilty of great neglect of their wives in this respect.

So also are those who banish all religious exercises from their houses, making their houses brothels of the devil rather than churches of God. If, for lack of means, either public or private, a wife lives and dies in ignorance, profaneness, unbelief and impenitence, which bring eternal damnation, her blood will assuredly be required at his hands. For a husband #footnote[Ezek 3:18. #emph[etc.]] is God’s watchman to his wife.

=== §. 49. #emph[Of a husband’s providing things needful for his wife’s body.]

A husband’s provident care for his wife must also extend to her body, and that both in health and in sickness. In health, by providing such things as are needful to preserve health, such as sufficient food, clothing and the like necessities. Where the #footnote[Isa 4:1.] prophet, to heighten the misery of the people, says, “Seven women will take hold of one man,” saying, “We will eat our own bread and provide our own clothes. Just let us be called by your name,” he implies that it was a husband’s duty to provide bread and clothing, that is, all necessities, for his wife. The #footnote[Exod 21:10.] law also implies this, where it commands the man who takes one wife on top of another not to diminish the food and clothing of the first. In sickness, such things are to be provided as are needful either to restore her health, or to comfort, cherish and refresh her in her sickness.

This was #footnote[Treatise 2, Part 2, §. 29.] noted before among the mutual duties common to both; for by virtue of the marriage bond it belongs to both man and wife. But it belongs to the man by virtue of the power and charge he has over his wife, and therefore it was needful to touch on it here.

=== §. 50. #emph[Of a husband’s provident care for his wife in her childbearing.]#footnote[Treatise 3, §. 49.]

Most fitting to this place is the provident care that husbands ought to have for their wives both before and at the time of their labor and their recovery after childbirth; and that in two things especially.

1\. In getting for their wives, to the utmost of their power and ability, such things as may satisfy their cravings, if they do have cravings (as in all ages women have been subject to them at the time of pregnancy and childbearing). For it is well known that it is very dangerous both for mother and child for her to go without what she craves: sometimes the death of the one, sometimes of the other, sometimes of both has followed from it.

2\. In providing such things as are needful for their labor and their recovery after childbirth. This time is especially to be provided for, for many reasons.

1\. Because it is a time of weakness, in which the woman cannot well provide for herself.

2\. Because her weakness is joined with much pain. The #footnote[The pains of women’s labor.] pain of a woman in labor is the greatest pain that is ordinarily endured by anyone for its time. None know it so well as those who feel it, and many husbands, because they are not subject to it, think only lightly of it. But if we duly weigh #footnote[Ps 48:6. Isa 13:8; 21:3. Jer 4:31; 30:6. Mic 4:9.] that the Holy Spirit, when he would set out the extremity of any pains and pangs, likens them to the pains of a woman in labor, we may well gather that they are the greatest of all. This is further shown by the shrieks and outcries that not only weak and faint-hearted women utter at the time of their labor, but that are also forced from the strongest and bravest women there are, and that even though they resolve beforehand to the contrary. Nor can we wonder at it; for their body is, as it were, set on a rack (at least if the labor is hard), and all their parts so stretched that it is a wonder they should ever recover their health and strength again, or that they should hold out against the brunt and not die in their labor, as Rachel and the wife of Phinehas#footnote[Gen 35:16, #emph[etc.] 1 Sam 4:19, 20.] and many in all ages have done. Surely among ordinary deliverances I know none so near a miracle, none in which the Almighty so evidently shows his great power and good providence, as in the safe delivery of women. Besides the great pang of labor, women are also subject after their delivery to many #emph[after-pains,] which are very painful. From all these pains, and the great weakness that befalls women after childbirth, especially if they nurse their children, men are freed because of their sex. Now then, to apply this point: seeing women are brought to such pains and weakness in bringing forth those children who are the man’s as well as hers, and he is freed from all of it, is it not very just and fitting that he should provide all things needful for her welfare, ease and recovery of strength?

3\. Because the lack of needful things is very dangerous at that time: dangerous to the health and life of the woman, and of the child also.

=== §. 51. #emph[Of neglecting wives in their weakness.]

Contrary to a husband’s provident care in general are those vices that were reproved in the treatise of common duties, #footnote[See Treatise 2, Part 2, §. 30, 39.] such as #emph[grudging the expense spent on a wife, covetousness, wastefulness] and #emph[idleness.]

But contrary in particular to a husband’s care for his wife in childbirth is the inhuman and worse than barbarous unkindness of many husbands, who give no thought at all to the weakness of their wives in this case, to help, ease and comfort them, but rather make their burden much heavier. For,

1\. Some, through covetousness, refuse beforehand to give their wife the means to provide such things as are needful for herself and her child. And when the time comes, if their wife wants a midwife who costs somewhat more than the nearest one, she will have none if she will not have the nearest. And as for a nurse to tend her, they think their maid will serve well enough: they need not go to the expense of bringing a nurse into the house. As for fitting lodging, some will not hesitate to say, “Can my wife not give birth in a room without a fireplace as well as the virgin Mary? Why should my wife need more things than she did?” Indeed, further, there are many who, when the time of their wife’s delivery draws near, carry her away from all her family to a place where she is not known, so that her family will not by their pleading draw him to spend and lay out more on his wife than he is willing to. While their wife is weak after childbirth, many are unwilling to allow their wives any other food than is provided in the house for themselves and their children, not considering that her appetite cannot be like theirs.

Many other such bitter fruits of unkind husbands, springing from covetousness, might be listed, by which husbands plainly show that they love their wealth better than their wives: they would rather lose #emph[them] than part with #emph[that.]

2\. Others, through jealous suspicion, do not refrain, even at #footnote[#emph[Si quid vxor peccauerit consolare & noli moerorem augere. Chrys. hom.] 26. #emph[in] 1 Cor 11. \[If your wife has done anything wrong, comfort her, and do not add to her grief.\]] the time of their wives’ pain and weakness, from reproaching them with looseness and saying that the child is not the husband’s. To lay this to a wife’s charge unjustly is at any time a most shameful and hateful reproach; but at the time of childbirth, whether just or unjust, it is a thing far too spiteful and vengeful. Some wives are so overcome by it (especially at the time of their weakness) that they are not able to bear it, but even faint and die under the reproach; others, of a bolder spirit, vow never to lie with their husbands again. Many such injuries follow from such unkindness.

=== §. 52. #emph[Of a husband’s providing for his wife according to his means and ability.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 3, §. 38.]

In a husband’s providing for his wife’s body, regard must be had to the #emph[measure] and to the #emph[manner.]

The #emph[measure] must extend as far as his ability. For a husband ought to maintain his wife in as good a condition and style as himself. By marriage she is advanced to as high a standing and dignity in relation to others as he is; and for her own use she is made a partner of all his goods, and accordingly ought to share in them.

As for the #emph[manner,] he must allow her (at least if he sees that she has sufficient discretion) to order such things as are needful for herself as she likes best; as Elkanah, in another case, said to his wife, “Do what you think is best.” #footnote[1 Sam 1:23.]

Both in the #emph[measure] and in the #emph[manner] of providing, a difference must be made between a wife and servants or children. These may have their portions of food, clothing and like necessities measured out and rationed to them, which is not fitting to be done to a wife. Nor is it needful that so plentiful a provision be made for them as for her.

=== §. 53. #emph[Of a husband’s stinginess toward his wife.]

Contrary to this is a husband’s stingy dealing with his wife: when the allowance she has is far below his means, and is also given to her little by little, as if she were a child. Many husbands make their wives drudge at home, eat poorly and dress shabbily, while they themselves are finely dressed, merry at their feasts away from home, and so far outdo their wives that they are ashamed to be seen in company with their wives. Those who marry their maids, or others of lower rank than themselves, often deal with them in this way, regarding them as no more than servants and lowly persons, though they are their wives. But it has been #footnote[§. 6.] shown before that wives are advanced by marriage to their husbands’ dignity, however lowly they were before.

=== §. 54. #emph[Of husbands’ allowing their wives to give to others, as they see good occasion.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 3, §. 23. 29. 33.]

A husband’s provident care for his wife ought to extend so far that she may have (besides what is needful for herself) something to give to those to whom it is fitting for her to give: namely, to children and servants in the house, and to others also outside the house. For this much is noted in Solomon’s description #footnote[Prov 31:15. #emph[etc. v.] 21. #emph[v.] 28.] of a good wife: “She provides food for her household and portions for her maidservants … all her household is clothed in scarlet” (namely, by her ordering and managing of things). “Her children rise up and call her blessed,” for her general conduct in the family, and also for the particular kindnesses she has shown them. As for #footnote[#emph[v.] 20.] others outside the house, it is also noted that “she opens her arms to the poor and reaches out her hands to the needy.” She did these things by virtue of the power and liberty her husband gave her, as appears from two points noted there:

1\. Before any mention is made of the things #footnote[#emph[v.] 11.] she did, it is said, “The heart of her husband trusts in her.”

2\. After all her good deeds are listed, it is #footnote[#emph[v.] 28.] said, “Her husband praises her.”

It is fitting that other husbands (whose wives are wise and faithful) should deal with their wives after this pattern: so that in the house the wives may have the more honor from children and servants, and outside the house they may give the better proof of their charity.

For when we consider the many excellent promises that are made to works of mercy and charity, and the many terrible threats that are denounced not only against those who practice cruelty, but also against those who show no mercy, and consider also that wives, together with their husbands, are “heirs of the #footnote[1 Pet 3:7.] grace of life,” it is very needful, indeed even necessary, that they should show their faith by some work of mercy and charity. Now unless her husband gives her something to bestow on others at her own discretion, no true and thorough proof of her merciful and charitable mind can be made. If she gives from what her husband has kept back for himself, then, as her giving is unlawful, so she may be thought generous not because she is merciful, but because, for all her generosity, she parts with nothing of her own. Indeed, even if she has a general consent to give from the common goods of the family as she sees cause, that is still not so sure and sound a proof of her charity and mercifulness as if she had something of her own, which she might keep or give away as she pleases, and what she does not give away, lay up as her own store, belonging to herself alone. For there is naturally such a self-love in man, and such a desire to keep what is one’s own, that he is very unwilling to part with any of it, unless conscience and grace alter this corruption of nature and so move him readily to lay out something for charitable uses. But otherwise, with what belongs in whole or in part to another (whether that other is a husband, parent, master, friend or anyone else) he is easily moved to be generous and bountiful: a man will gladly cut a wide strap (as we say) out of another man’s leather.

It is known that many children and servants who, when they come to possess their own goods, are very stingy and miserly, have been generous to the poor with their parents’ and masters’ goods. Indeed, partners in a joint stock will be much more eager to give away what they hold in common with another than what belongs to each of them alone. The truest proof of a merciful and charitable heart lies in giving out what is one’s own.

It is therefore fitting, on this very ground, that a husband should, according to his ability, let his wife have some fund and portion of her own, free for her to dispose of as she sees good; letting her know that the chief end for which he provides so plentifully for her is that she may show the fruits of her faith by some works of charity, and urging her to do so. Many religious, wise and kind husbands do this: some give their wives a quarterly allowance in money; others give their wives power to receive a certain portion of rent from certain lands or houses; others settle on their wives full ownership of some heritable property, and allow them to receive its profits and revenues; others give them certain fees from their offices or from their trade; others, who are poor, allow them to work for themselves and dispose of their earnings as they see cause. Some take one way, some another. Every man in his place knows best the means by which to please his wife in this matter; it will be enough for me to have laid down the general rule.

=== §. 55. #emph[Of husbands’ too great tight-fistedness toward their wives.]

Contrary to this is the tight-fistedness of those who allow their wives no more than may serve for their own private use. They think it a great matter, and as much as a husband is bound to do, to let her have clothing, food and drink, and such necessities as befit her rank; but anything beyond that they think needless. So their wives are deprived not only of means to gain respect from their children and servants at home, and to reward those who are obedient and ready to serve them, but also of means to perform such works of mercy as opportunity requires and their conscience moves them to do. Indeed, many wives of rich husbands are brought to great shame by it: for being in places where there is just occasion to contribute to some charitable use, and where, because of their rich and costly clothing, they are expected to be generous, they have nothing at all to give. The fault of some husbands in this respect is great in many ways: 1. They bring shame and grief to their wives, whom they ought to regard with all tenderness. 2. They dishonor their own standing: for those who take notice of this tight-fistedness toward their wives will be ready to judge them both covetous and unkind. 3. The leaving undone of the work of mercy that their wives should have done will be laid to their charge. They will hear that dreadful sentence, “Depart, you#super[+] who are cursed, into the eternal fire,” for “I was hungry and you#super[+] gave Me nothing to eat, etc.” And if they answer, “When did we see You hungry, etc.,” it will be replied, “Because you#super[+] did not allow your#super[+] wives to do it, you#super[+] did not do it.”

So much for the #emph[extent] of a husband’s provident care for the good of his wife. Next we must speak of its continuance.

=== §. 57. #emph[Of a husband’s care to provide for his wife as long as she lives.]

The #emph[continuance] of a husband’s provident care for his wife must last as long as she lives, even if she outlives him. Not that he can actually provide for her when he is dead, but that before his death he may so provide for her that she may have the means to maintain herself, and to live according to the station to which she is advanced by him; at least, that he leave her not only as much as he had with her, but something more also, as a testimony of his love to her and care for her. Husbands have the example of Christ to press this duty on them: for when he went away from his church#footnote[Eph 4:8.] here on earth, he left his Spirit, who furnished her with gifts as plentifully as if Christ had remained with her, if not more abundantly.

For the better performance of this duty, husbands who die before their wives must observe two things especially, among others.

1\. That they declare their mind and #footnote[Husbands, before they die, must make their will, even for their wives’ good.] will plainly and expressly before they die, so that their wives are not cheated and defrauded of what they intended for them. David did this at the request of Bathsheba: he settled his estate, and had Bathsheba’s son actually crowned before he himself breathed his last. He did this for other weighty reasons, but in particular for his wife’s good, as may be gathered from the reason she put to the king in these #footnote[1 Kgs 1:21. Husbands on their deathbed must commend their wives to some faithful friend. John 19:26, 27. #emph[Reason.] The truest proof of love at parting.] words: “Otherwise, when my lord the king rests with his fathers, I will be counted as a criminal.”

2\. That he ask some faithful friend to be a helper to her in his place (as Christ commended his mother to his disciple John). This will be needful because of her weakness, by reason of her sex, and her lack of experience in managing affairs, especially those outside the house.

At the time of a man’s departure out of this world from his wife, the truest proof of his love for his wife will be given. For many who keep up a fair show toward their wives while they live with them show at their death that there was no soundness of affection in their heart toward them: it was all a mere show, for some ulterior motives.

=== §. 58. #emph[Of husbands’ neglect of their wives’ future condition.]

Contrary to this are various practices of unkind husbands. For #footnote[Contrary. 1. A willful inability.]

1\. Some, through lack of foresight, thriftlessness and wastefulness, disable themselves from doing good to their wives after their death; and so they leave their wives nothing, or (what is worse than nothing) in debt, and with many children to provide for. The care husbands ought to have for their wives should make them think beforehand of the time to come, and even for their wives’ sake be somewhat more diligent, thrifty and provident, and cut off many unnecessary expenses; otherwise their sin is doubled: 1. By a needless wasting of their estate. 2. By neglecting their wives.

2\. Others, by flattering or forcing means, draw their wives #footnote[2\. Making away with their wives’ jointure, or any other right.] to give up the right they have in money, goods, house or land by jointure, inheritance or any other way, and yet make them no sufficient recompense in another kind; but at their death leave their wives in a far worse condition than they were in before marriage, besides a greater charge than they had before. As this is a great piece of unkindness, so it is also a main point of injustice.

3\. Others, grudging against the laws under which they #footnote[3\. Defeating of #emph[thirds.]] live for providing for a wife by #emph[thirds] (a third part of the husband’s estate) or otherwise, use every fraudulent means they can to deprive her of what the law would otherwise settle on her. The civil laws of the state where we live ought to be the rule of our civil actions (so far as they are not contrary to God’s word), and we ought, “as a matter of conscience,” to be subject to #footnote[Rom 13:5.] them. Besides, a husband ought (even if the law did not force him) to leave at least the #emph[thirds] to his wife, as a testimony of his love to her and care for her. So this also is a double fault: 1. A transgression of the law. 2. A mark of unkindness.

4\. Others, having aged and sickly wives, or otherwise #footnote[4\. Making no will.] thinking that their wives may, or rather hoping that their wives will, die before themselves, put off making their wills on purpose, so that they need not put in their wives’ #emph[thirds,] but may dispose of them some other way. Besides showing no good affection toward their wives, these husbands provoke God to disappoint them of their hopes; and so he often does. For he takes them away before their wives, and takes them away in such a way, with no time to make their will, that not only do their wives enjoy their #emph[thirds] (which the husbands so much wanted to avoid), but someone else also (whom of all people they disliked in their lifetime) seizes on the other two parts.

=== §. 58. #emph[Of a husband’s protecting his wife from danger.]

Having shown how a husband is to provide what is needful for his wife, it remains to show how he is to protect her from what is harmful.

Because of the #emph[protection] a husband owes his wife, he is called “the veil of her eyes.”#footnote[Gen 20:16.] As this phrase implies #emph[subjection] on the wife’s part, so it also implies #emph[protection] on the husband’s. To protect someone is, as it were, to #emph[cover] them, namely, from danger; to be negligent and careless of them is, as it were, to lay them open to danger. The same duty is implied under another similar phrase, of #emph[spreading his wing] over his wife. #footnote[Ruth 3:9.] The metaphor is taken from birds, which spread their wings over their young to keep them from harm. This phrase and metaphor is also applied to God, to #footnote[Ruth 2:12.] set out his protection.

But most pertinent to this purpose is the title #footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 1, §. 15. and Treatise 3, §. 73.] #emph[savior,] given to a husband in relation to his wife.

For this end the Lord, who made a woman subject to her husband, gave his sex greater strength, courage and boldness than hers, so that he might protect her who is the #emph[weaker vessel.] In this duty of #emph[protection] Christ shows himself an excellent pattern and precedent to husbands.

To perform this duty better, a husband must be careful,

1\. To guard against, as far as he can, such dangers as his wife is likely to fall into.

2\. To recover her from such as she has fallen into.

For this purpose David took his wives into Gath,#footnote[1 Sam 27:3.] so that, if they were left in Israel, Saul would not do them any harm; and again, when they were taken by the Amalekites, he recovered #footnote[1 Sam 30:18.] them.

A husband’s care in protecting his wife must be shown according to the danger to which wives are subject.

1\. If she is in danger of being led astray and enticed, as Eve was, #footnote[A wife must be kept from being led astray.] by any evil instruments of the devil, such as Jesuits, priests, friars, or profane, blasphemous, lustful or dissolute persons, his care must be either to keep them away so that they do not come to her, or to put them away from her as soon as he can. He must not allow them to lodge in his house.

2\. If by any trick she is drawn from his house, he must #footnote[A wife stolen away must be brought home. Judg 19:2. 2 Sam 3:13, 14. 1 Sam 30:18. A wife’s good name must be upheld.] seek her and bring her back again, as the Levite did his wife; or have her (if he can) brought home again, as David had Michal brought; especially if they are taken away by force, as Ahinoam and Abigail, David’s wives, were.

3\. If she is unjustly slandered, he is to uphold her good name and reputation as much as his own: as Christ counts himself despised when his church is, so must he. He must have this care #footnote[See Treatise 2, Part 2, §. 31. etc.] for his wife’s good name even after her death, as well as while she lives.

4\. Whatever other injury is intended or practiced against #footnote[Luke 10:16. A wife must be defended against strangers, servants and children. Esth 7:7, 8. #emph[etc.]] her, he must be a tower of defense to protect her (as Ahasuerus was to Esther against Haman), and that not only against strangers outside the house, but also against children and servants in the house. Grown children who are proud and stubborn will be ready to rise up against their mother, especially if she is a stepmother, because she is the weaker sex; for the most part, a father’s look keeps them most in awe. Therefore the husband must be a help to his wife and uphold her honor against them, even if they are children of a former wife.

=== §. 59. #emph[Of a husband’s defending his wife against children of a former wife, and servants.]

#emph[Objection.] Stepmothers often prove unkind and unjust, and deal unmercifully with their husbands’ children. Must a husband in such cases support his wife against his children?

#emph[Answer.] The protection I speak of is for the case where a wife is wronged; then her husband is to do what he can to right her (as we say). But if she is the wrongdoer, he must by no means back her up against his children, and so make their wrong the greater. Yet he ought to respect his wife so far as to labor, by all the fair means he can, to calm her mind and turn her heart toward them. And if he sees that her heart is wholly turned against them, then he is to send them out to be brought up in some other place, and so take away from her the object of her displeasure, so that he and she may live together more quietly. For if a man must “leave his father and mother,” he must also leave his children, and “be joined to his wife.” Peace and unity between man and wife must be kept unbroken above all others. “Though you cast away #footnote[#emph[Licèt omnia proijcias, nihil molestius continget quàm non habere beneuolam domi vxorem: peccatum nullum magis dolendum inueneris, quàm cum vxore seditionem habere. Chrysost. hom.] 26. #emph[in] 1 Cor 11. Gen 16:6; 21:14.] all, nothing can happen more troublesome to you than not to have a quiet wife at home. You can find no sin more grievous than to have strife with a wife.”

If a wife must be defended against the stubbornness of children, much more against the insolence of servants. For this purpose the example of Abraham is recorded: his servant might have had a privilege above others, because he had taken her to his bed; yet when she grew insolent toward her mistress, he first put her into her mistress’s hand, to do with her as her mistress pleased, and afterward he cast her out of his house.

=== §. 60. #emph[Of neglecting to defend their wives.]

Contrary to this is a dissolute carelessness of husbands, who care no more to help and relieve their wives than they would anyone else.

1\. Some fear offending their wives more than they care to do #footnote[Contrary: dissolute carelessness. 1. In allowing deceivers to come to the house.] them good, and on that account they let any sort of people come to their wives who are welcome to the wives. If magistrates in a commonwealth will answer for allowing deceivers to come into their dominions to lead their people astray, much more will husbands answer for allowing them to come and lead their wives astray.

#[
#set enum(indent: 24pt)
+ Because husbands have a greater charge over their wives than magistrates have over their people.
+ Because wives ought to be dearer to husbands than people to magistrates.
+ Because husbands can spot such men sooner in their house than magistrates can in the commonwealth.
+ Because such men can be kept out of a house much more easily than out of a commonwealth or a city.
]

2\. Others do not care where their wives wander; and if their wives#footnote[2\. In allowing wives to go wherever they please.] do go out of the house, they will never be sought after by their husbands. Though this may be a just punishment on wandering wives, yet it is not just for husbands to deal with them so. If Christ our husband dealt with us so, we would soon be lost: for we often go astray like wandering sheep, but he is that good shepherd who seeks the lost sheep until he #footnote[Luke 15:4.] finds it.

3\. No wonder, then, that many husbands are no more moved #footnote[3\. In disregarding ill reports about wives.] by the ill reports and rumors raised against their wives, when they care so little who comes to their wives or where their wives go. Assuredly the discredit of a wife will turn to the man’s dishonor: for as “a wife of noble character is her husband’s crown,”#footnote[Prov 12:4.] so by the rule of contraries, an infamous wife is a shame to her husband. If therefore not for his wife’s sake, then for his own sake a man ought not to pass over too carelessly the ill reports that are raised against his wife.

4\. There are husbands so unkind as to be moved by no ill #footnote[4\. In allowing wives to be mistreated.] treatment done to their wives, and they will not hear any complaint the wives make to them. Indeed, if they see their wives mistreated, they will either pretend not to see it, or only smile at it and go their way, and leave their wives to right themselves as well as they can. As it does not become any Christian to allow his neighbor to be wronged (for it is noted as a commendable thing in Moses that #footnote[Exod 2:13.] when he saw two Hebrews fighting, he took the part of the one who was wronged and reproved the other), so much less does it become a husband, to whose safekeeping his wife is committed. Nature teaches us that the head is as much affected by a wrong done to the body as by one done to itself; so ought a husband to be. #footnote[5\. In backing up children or servants against wives.]

5\. As the wrong done by those who are in subjection under the wife in the house is greater than that done by strangers, so the husband’s fault in allowing it is the greater; for he has more power over them in his house than over others. #footnote[Si illam quae secundum post te locum obtinet contumelia afficis, non parum tui principatus honori derogas. Chrys. loc. cit. \[If you treat with insult her who holds the second place after you, you take no little from the honor of your own rule.\]] What then may we think of those who, either by looking the other way or by taking sides against their wives, allow both children and servants to treat their wives with scorn? Assuredly those husbands will themselves get some taste of its bitter and evil fruit; and that not only through the great discontent their wives must take at it, but also through the contempt that will fall on their own persons, both from their wives (who cannot think them fit heads to govern others) and from their children and servants, who will take occasion from it to grow proud and presumptuous against him. By despising the weaker, men grow little by little to despise the stronger. Men of wisdom and experience know this well; and so in commonwealths and states governed by wise men, the authority of inferior magistrates is upheld and maintained. Superior magistrates will not allow those who are in authority under them to be despised; for it is well known that it serves not only the honor and ease but also the safety of the supreme magistrate to have the power and authority of inferior magistrates respected, and not trampled underfoot. It shows, therefore, a lack both of love and of discretion and understanding in husbands to allow child, servant or anyone else in the house to treat their wives with scorn, who are joint governors with them over the house.
